        'By four-thirty on a November afternoon in Srinagar, before streetlamps flicker along the Jhelum bund or rush-hour minibuses clear the bottlenecks at Jahangir Chowk and Hyderpora, the sky performs a familiar seasonal contraction. Across residential lanes from Rajbagh and Rawalpora to Soura, sweepers and householders touch matches to heaped pyramids of dry Chinar and poplar foliage. Fifteen kilometres south and west across the karewa terraces of Pulwama, Shopian, and Budgam, thin ribbons of grey-white smoke curl upward from pruned apple-orchard twigs and the tall stacks of early-firing brick kilns, while east of Pampore a pale calcareous veil hangs above the limestone quarries and clinker rotary kilns of Khrew and Wuyan. As solar heating fades from the valley floor and katabatic cold air slides down the Pir Panjal and Zabarwan flanks, those disparate plumes merge into a single acrid ceiling trapped two hundred metres overhead.',
