        'Nothing corrodes public trust—or muddies well-intentioned civil-society webinars—more severely than Kashmir’s unresolved metrological schism. Log onto three different air-quality portals on a foggy November morning in Srinagar, and a citizen is confronted with three irreconcilable atmospheres. An official Continuous Ambient Air Quality Monitoring Station (CAAQMS) feed at Rajbagh may report a calm “Moderate” National Air Quality Index (NAQI) near 115; private crowd-sourced optical sensor dashboards in Shalateng, Hyderpora, or Kanipora simultaneously flash alarming purple banners between 340 and 480; while global satellite-assimilated reanalysis maps paint the entire district a uniform, bland amber.',
