        'Turn from dispersed rural bonfires to stationary point sources, and the gap between statutory notifications and field enforcement widens further. Across Budgam district—Chadoora, Ichgam, Ompora, Dreygam, and Magam—as well as Pulwama and Anantnag, hundreds of brick kilns (pajakhs) ring Srinagar’s upwind southern and western perimeter, while east of Pampore the Khrew–Wuyan industrial cluster concentrates rotary cement kilns, limestone mines, and stone crushers.',
        'On paper, India’s brick-kiln regulatory regime underwent a major overhaul on February 22, 2022, when MoEFCC notified G.S.R. 143(E) under the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986—mandating that existing Fixed Chimney Bull’s Trench Kilns (FCBTKs) convert within two years to Induced-Draft Zig-Zag technology, vertical shaft kilns, or clean gaseous fuels, while capping stack particulate matter at 250 mg/Nm³ (normalized to 4 percent CO2) and prohibiting operation without valid J&K PCC Consent to Operate. Litigation before the National Green Tribunal Principal Bench (including Original Application No. 89/2021, tracked in Kashmir EcoWatch’s judicial registry as kew-case-008) reinforced siting buffer norms near orchards, habitations, and karewa scarps.',
        'Field combustion science, however, exposes why paper “zig-zag conversion certificates” frequently fail to deliver clean air. Comparative stack-emission analyses between zig-zag and traditional FCBTK kilns demonstrate that zig-zag airflow geometry reduces particulate and black-carbon emissions only when paired with continuous induced-draft fan operation, crushed dry coal of controlled sulfur/ash content, and disciplined single-man stoking intervals (Hasnain et al., 2026). When kiln operators—facing thin margins or lax autumn–spring inspections—burn high-sulfur coal fines, petroleum coke, wet sawmill waste, or unsegregated scrap, incomplete smouldering returns, depositing heavy metals into surrounding soils and shallow groundwater around J&K brick-kiln belts (Mohan et al., 2024) while stripping irreplaceable Pleistocene karewa loess for brick earth (Maske, 2026).',
        'Parallel enforcement gaps afflict the Khrew cement and stone-crushing belt. Agronomic field investigations around Khrew document measurable alkalinization, crust formation, and nutrient disruption in agricultural and saffron soils exposed to chronic cement-dust fallout (Rasheed et al., 2025), alongside biochemical foliar stress in trees bordering central Kashmir stone crushers (Hamid et al., 2023). Although major cement plants are nominally required to operate electrostatic precipitators or baghouse filters linked to Continuous Emission Monitoring Systems (CEMS), raw hourly CEMS stack streams from Khrew and Wuyan are not published on a citizen-accessible public dashboard allowing independent verification during nocturnal filter bypasses or power fluctuations.',
